var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_introduction/#Introduction:-individual-based-realizations","page":"Introduction","text":"This package realizes structured population models as individual-based models (IBMs) on the Ark.jl entity-component-system. Each individual is an entity carrying a continuous Size trait; birth spawns an entity, death despawns one, and growth updates the trait. The IBM is the finite- N ground truth whose large-N limit is the deterministic IPM, while also capturing demographic stochasticity and extinction.","title":"Introduction: individual-based realizations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_introduction/#Vital-rates-and-domain","page":"Introduction","text":"We reuse the IntegralProjectionModels.jl vital-rate types. Here survival and per-capita fecundity are size-independent (so the deterministic total is exact for any mesh), while growth and recruitment are genuine distributions over the trait.\n\nusing IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics\nusing IntegralProjectionModels\nusing StructuredPopulationCore: meshpoints, step_size,\n    sample_survives, sample_growth, offspring_count\nusing Random, Statistics, Plots\n\nsurvival  = LinearSurvival(1.0, 0.0)              # logistic(1.0) ≈ 0.73\ngrowth    = NormalGrowth(0.5, 0.8, 0.4)           # z' ~ Normal(0.5 + 0.8 z, 0.4)\nfecundity = FecundityRate(-1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.3, 1.0)  # ≈ 0.37 offspring; recruits ~ Normal(1, 0.3)\ndomain    = ContinuousDomain(0.0, 6.0, 60)\n\nThe samplers turn these descriptions into per-individual draws:\n\nrng = Random.Xoshiro(2024)\n(survives   = sample_survives(rng, survival, 2.0),\n grown      = sample_growth(rng, growth, 2.0, domain),\n n_offspring = offspring_count(rng, fecundity, 2.0, domain))\n(survives = true, grown = 1.6083019669659286, n_offspring = 0)","title":"Vital rates and domain"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_introduction/#Building-and-running-an-IBM","page":"Introduction","text":"N0 = 5000\ntraits0 = clamp.(2.0 .+ 0.5 .* randn(rng, N0), 0.01, 5.99)\n\nworld = ibm_world(survival, growth, fecundity, domain;\n                  rng = rng, traits0 = collect(traits0))\nres = ibm_run!(world, 6; save_traits = true)\nres.N\n7-element Vector{Int64}:\n 5000\n 5463\n 6075\n 6686\n 7325\n 7977\n 8780\n\nplot(0:6, res.N; marker = :circle, legend = false,\n     xlabel = \"time step\", ylabel = \"population size\",\n     title = \"Individual-based population trajectory\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"Building and running an IBM"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_introduction/#Comparison-with-the-deterministic-IPM","page":"Introduction","text":"Seeding the deterministic IPM from the same initial individuals (binned onto the mesh), the IBM tracks it.\n\nkernel = PKernel(survival, growth, domain) + FKernel(fecundity, domain)\nz = meshpoints(domain)\nh = step_size(domain)\n\nn0 = zeros(length(z))\nfor zt in traits0\n    n0[clamp(floor(Int, (zt - domain.lower) / h) + 1, 1, length(z))] += 1\nend\ndetsol = solve(IPMProblem(kernel, domain, n0, (0, 6)), DirectIteration())\ndet_total = [sum(u) for u in detsol.u]\n\nplot(0:6, det_total; label = \"deterministic IPM\", lw = 2)\nplot!(0:6, res.N; seriestype = :scatter, label = \"IBM realization\",\n      xlabel = \"time step\", ylabel = \"population size\",\n      title = \"IBM vs deterministic total\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nThe trait distribution matches too:\n\nibm_hist = trait_histogram(world, domain)\nplot(z, detsol.u[end]; label = \"deterministic density\", lw = 2)\nplot!(z, ibm_hist; seriestype = :steppre, label = \"IBM histogram\",\n      xlabel = \"trait z\", ylabel = \"count\", title = \"Trait distribution at t = 6\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}","title":"Comparison with the deterministic IPM"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_introduction/#Eviction-and-small-populations","page":"Introduction","text":"Traits that would leave the domain are handled by an eviction policy (:reflect, :clamp, or :discard); all individuals stay in-domain:\n\nall(0.0 .<= res.traits[end] .<= 6.0)\ntrue\n\nBecause the IBM tracks discrete individuals, small sub-critical populations can go extinct — behaviour the deterministic density cannot represent:\n\npoor_survival = LinearSurvival(-1.0, 0.0)          # logistic(-1) ≈ 0.27\nno_recruits   = FecundityRate(-50.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.3, 1.0)\nsmall = ibm_world(poor_survival, growth, no_recruits, domain;\n                  rng = Random.Xoshiro(7), traits0 = fill(2.0, 30))\nibm_run!(small, 40).N[end]   # extinct\n0","title":"Eviction and small populations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Discrete-time-IBM","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Discrete-time IBM"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Continuous-time-IBM-(measure-valued-PDMP)","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Continuous-time IBM (measure-valued PDMP)"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Super-individuals","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Super-individuals"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Stage-structure-(pure-jump-finite-state-IBM)","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Stage structure (pure-jump finite-state IBM)"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Components-and-resources","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Components and resources"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#SciML-problem-and-integrator","page":"API Reference","text":"An individual-based model as a SciML problem, so that solve, ensembles and simulation-based resilience metrics (recovery, basin_stability and the early-warning indicators of DynamicalResilience.jl) apply to it. The state is the population binned on the mesh (or counted by stage).","title":"SciML problem and integrator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_world","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_world(survival, growth, fecundity, domain; rng, traits0, eviction=:reflect)\n\nBuild an Ark World of Size entities for an individual-based realization, with the vital rates, RNG, domain, and eviction policy stored as resources and one entity spawned per value in traits0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_world"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_step!","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_step!(world)\n\nAdvance one discrete time step. Within a single Query (which locks the world) each individual's offspring and survival/growth are resolved against its current trait; structural changes (deaths, births) are buffered and applied after the query completes. The conditional mean reproduces the deterministic IPM kernel K = P + F.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_step!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_run!","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_run!(world, n_steps; save_traits=false) -> (; N, traits)\n\nRun n_steps discrete steps in place. Returns the population-size trajectory N (length n_steps+1, including the initial size) and, if save_traits, the trait vectors at each saved time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_run!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.population_size","page":"API Reference","text":"population_size(world) -> Int\n\nNumber of living individuals. Counts via a completed Query iteration (which releases the world lock — count_entities(Query(...)) would leave it locked).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.population_size"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.traits","page":"API Reference","text":"traits(world) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nTrait values of all living individuals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.traits"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.trait_histogram","page":"API Reference","text":"trait_histogram(world, domain) -> Vector{Int}\n\nCounts of living individuals per mesh bin of domain (for comparison with the deterministic density).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.trait_histogram"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.CTVitalRates","page":"API Reference","text":"Per-individual continuous-time vital rates: deterministic growth flow(x), mortality(x) death rate, fecundity(x) birth rate, and recruit(rng) returning an offspring trait. (Non-parametric for Ark resource keying.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.CTVitalRates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_world_ct","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_world_ct(flow, mortality, fecundity, recruit, domain; rng, traits0, eviction=:reflect)\n\nBuild an Ark World for a continuous-time individual-based realization (a measure-valued PDMP): individuals flow deterministically by flow(x) and undergo death (rate mortality(x)) and birth (rate fecundity(x), offspring trait from recruit(rng)) jumps. This is the individual-level dual of a PSPM transport problem (flow=velocity, mortality=mortality, births=boundary/fecundity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_world_ct"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_step_ct!","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_step_ct!(world, dt)\n\nAdvance continuous time by dt with an operator split: over [t, t+dt] each individual produces Poisson(fecundity(x)·dt) offspring, dies with probability 1 - exp(-mortality(x)·dt), and otherwise flows to x + flow(x)·dt (Euler). Exact as dt → 0; the mean obeys the transport/​generator equation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_step_ct!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_run_ct!","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_run_ct!(world, tspan; dt, saveat=dt, save_traits=false) -> (; t, N, traits)\n\nRun the continuous-time IBM over tspan with step dt, recording population size (and optionally traits) every saveat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_run_ct!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.Weight","page":"API Reference","text":"Super-individual weight: the number of real individuals a particle represents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.Weight"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_world_super","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_world_super(survival, growth, fecundity, domain; rng, traits0, weights0, eviction=:reflect)\n\nBuild an Ark World of weighted super-individuals (Size + Weight): each particle represents Weight.w real individuals at one trait. weights0 defaults to all-ones (the exact IBM). Demographic events use exact aggregate draws (Binomial survivors, Poisson offspring), so total-count statistics match the unweighted IBM while the entity count stays small.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_world_super"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_step_super!","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_step_super!(world)\n\nOne discrete step for weighted super-individuals. A particle (z, w) draws its aggregate offspring/survivor counts with Poisson(w · expected_offspring(z)) and Binomial(w, survival(z)), but each represented individual then gets an independent recruit/growth trait draw. This preserves the super-individual count-process statistics while restoring within-particle trait variance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_step_super!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_run_super!","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_run_super!(world, n_steps; merge_domain=nothing) -> (; N, particles)\n\nRun n_steps weighted steps, returning the total-count trajectory N and the particle-count trajectory. If merge_domain is a domain, merge_by_bin! is applied after each step to cap the particle count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_run_super!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.total_count","page":"API Reference","text":"total_count(world) -> Int\n\nTotal number of real individuals (sum of super-individual weights).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.total_count"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.n_particles","page":"API Reference","text":"n_particles(world) -> Int\n\nNumber of super-individual particles (entities). total_count(world) is the represented population.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.n_particles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.merge_by_bin!","page":"API Reference","text":"merge_by_bin!(world, domain) -> world\n\nCap particle count by merging all weight in each domain mesh bin into a single particle at the bin midpoint (total weight conserved; trait resolution = mesh).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.merge_by_bin!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.Stage","page":"API Reference","text":"Discrete stage of an individual (the individual-based analogue of a finite state). Multi-component individuals combine Stage with Size/Age.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.Stage"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.StageVitalRates","page":"API Reference","text":"Per-stage continuous-time rates: Qtrans[s', s] = rate of an s-individual transitioning to s' (off-diagonal movement), death[s] mortality rate, and birth matrix birth[s', s] = rate an s-individual produces an offspring in stage s'. (Non-parametric for Ark resource keying.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.StageVitalRates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_world_stage","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_world_stage(Qtrans, death, birth; rng, stages0)\n\nBuild an Ark World of Stage individuals for a stage-structured continuous-time IBM — a pure-jump Markov process whose mean is the finite-state generator dn/dt = G·n (with G assembled from the transition/death/birth rates; birth is the offspring matrix birth[to, from]). One entity is spawned per entry of stages0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_world_stage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_step_stage!","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_step_stage!(world, dt)\n\nOne operator-split step: each individual in stage s produces Poisson(birth[to, s]·dt) offspring in each stage to, and with probability 1 - exp(-R·dt) (R = total transition + death rate) undergoes one event — death, or a transition to s' with probability Qtrans[s', s] / R (an in-place stage change).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_step_stage!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_run_stage!","page":"API Reference","text":"ibm_run_stage!(world, tspan; dt, saveat=dt, n_stages) -> (; t, counts)\n\nRun the stage-structured IBM over tspan with step dt, recording the per-stage counts every saveat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.ibm_run_stage!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.stage_counts","page":"API Reference","text":"stage_counts(world, n_stages) -> Vector{Int}\n\nNumber of individuals in each stage 1:n_stages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.stage_counts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.Size","page":"API Reference","text":"Individual's continuous trait (e.g. size). Immutable — updated by write-back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.Size"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.RNGResource","page":"API Reference","text":"Resource holding the simulation RNG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.RNGResource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.VitalRates","page":"API Reference","text":"Resource holding the vital-rate objects (survival, growth, fecundity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.VitalRates"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.DomainResource","page":"API Reference","text":"Resource holding the trait domain (for eviction and the density-sampler fallback).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.DomainResource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.EvictionPolicy","page":"API Reference","text":"Resource selecting the out-of-domain policy: :reflect, :clamp, or :discard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.EvictionPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.IBMProblem","page":"API Reference","text":"IBMProblem(rates, u0, tspan; domain=nothing, dt=1, seed=0, eviction=:reflect)\n\nAn individual-based population model as a SciML problem. rates chooses the model:\n\nVitalRates(survival, growth, fecundity): the discrete-time size-structured IBM of ibm_world (one step per generation; dt must be 1);\nCTVitalRates(flow, mortality, fecundity, recruit): the continuous-time IBM of ibm_world_ct, stepped with dt;\nStageVitalRates(Qtrans, death, birth): the stage-structured IBM of ibm_world_stage, stepped with dt.\n\nState. The state u is a Vector{Float64}: the number of individuals in each of the domain.n_meshpoints equal bins of domain ([lower, upper], as in trait_histogram) for size-structured models, or in each stage for stage models. u0 is such a vector of counts; individuals are created from it with traits uniform within their bin, using the run's random number generator, so a run is a function of u0 and seed only. Non-integer counts are rounded stochastically (to the floor or the ceiling, with probabilities that keep the expected count).\n\nRandomness. Each run uses Random.Xoshiro(seed); reinit! restarts it, so reinitialising replays a run exactly.\n\nChanging the state. Assigning integ.u (then derivative_discontinuity!(integ, true)) or calling set_u!(integ, u) changes the population to the new counts before the next step: in a bin (or stage) that must lose individuals, that many are removed uniformly at random among those in it; in one that must gain, new individuals are added with traits uniform in the bin. So a perturbation acts at the resolution of the mesh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.IBMProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.IBMStepping","page":"API Reference","text":"IBMStepping()\n\nThe algorithm of an IBMProblem: advance the individual-based world one step at a time with the package's own stepping functions (ibm_step!, ibm_step_ct! or ibm_step_stage!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.IBMStepping"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.IBMIntegrator","page":"API Reference","text":"IBMIntegrator\n\nThe stepping state of an IBMProblem, created by init(prob, IBMStepping(); ...), with the interface of a SciML integrator:\n\ninteg.u: the binned (or staged) counts, a Vector{Float64}; integ.t: the time; integ.world: the Ark world of individuals.\nstep!(integ) (one step of dt), step!(integ, n) (n steps), step!(integ, dt, stop_at_tdt) (until the time has advanced by at least dt, or exactly dt, which must then be a whole number of steps), solve!(integ), reinit!(integ), set_u!(integ, u), terminate!(integ), check_error(integ).\nChanging integ.u in place takes effect before the next step once derivative_discontinuity!(integ, true) (or u_modified!) is called.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.IBMIntegrator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.IBMSolution","page":"API Reference","text":"IBMSolution\n\nThe result of solve(prob::IBMProblem, ...): the saved states u (binned or staged counts) at times t, the problem, and the retcode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.IBMSolution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.seed_trajectory","page":"API Reference","text":"seed_trajectory(prob::IBMProblem, ctx)\n\nThe default prob_func of an EnsembleProblem of an IBMProblem: trajectory i is remake(prob; seed=prob.seed + i), or uses the per-trajectory seed SciMLBase derives when the ensemble is solved with a seed keyword.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.seed_trajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CommonSolve.init-Tuple{IBMProblem}","page":"API Reference","text":"init(prob::IBMProblem, alg=IBMStepping(); seed=nothing, saveat=nothing,\n     save_everystep=saveat === nothing, save_start=true, save_end=true)\n\nSet up an IBMProblem for stepping. seed runs with that seed instead of the problem's; saveat is a spacing or a collection of times at which solve! records the state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.init"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CommonSolve.solve-Tuple{IBMProblem}","page":"API Reference","text":"solve(prob::IBMProblem, alg=IBMStepping(); kwargs...) -> IBMSolution\n\nRun the individual-based model over its tspan (init then solve!; keywords as for init).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.remake-Tuple{IBMProblem}","page":"API Reference","text":"remake(prob::IBMProblem; rates, u0, tspan, domain, dt, seed, eviction)\n\nA copy of the problem with some fields changed, for example remake(prob; seed=2) for another replicate or remake(prob; u0=n) for another initial population.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.remake"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.reinit!-Tuple{IBMIntegrator}","page":"API Reference","text":"reinit!(integ::IBMIntegrator, u0=integ.prob.u0; t0=integ.prob.tspan[1])\n\nRestart from the counts u0 at time t0 with a fresh world and the random number generator restarted from the problem's seed, discarding the saved states: reinitialising with the same u0 and t0 replays the run exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.reinit!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.set_u!-Tuple{IBMIntegrator, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"set_u!(integ::IBMIntegrator, u)\n\nChange the population to the counts u before the next step, by removing or adding individuals bin by bin (or stage by stage); see IBMProblem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.set_u!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.u_modified!-Tuple{IBMIntegrator, Bool}","page":"API Reference","text":"u_modified!(integ::IBMIntegrator, modified::Bool)\n\nTell the integrator whether integ.u was changed in place, so that the change is (or is not) applied to the individuals before the next step. derivative_discontinuity! (SciMLBase 3's name) does the same.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.u_modified!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.terminate!-Tuple{IBMIntegrator}","page":"API Reference","text":"terminate!(integ::IBMIntegrator, retcode=ReturnCode.Terminated)\n\nStop the run after the current step, for example when the population is extinct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.terminate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.check_error-Tuple{IBMIntegrator}","page":"API Reference","text":"check_error(integ::IBMIntegrator) -> ReturnCode.T\n\nReturnCode.Success while the run is going normally, else its return code (for example Terminated).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.check_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.successful_retcode-Tuple{IBMSolution}","page":"API Reference","text":"successful_retcode(sol::IBMSolution) -> Bool\n\nWhether the run ended normally (Success or Terminated).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.successful_retcode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.EnsembleProblem-Tuple{IBMProblem}","page":"API Reference","text":"EnsembleProblem(prob::IBMProblem; prob_func=seed_trajectory, kwargs...)\n\nAn ensemble of independent replicates: by default each trajectory gets its own seed (seed_trajectory). Solve it with solve(ens, IBMStepping(), EnsembleThreads(); trajectories).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.EnsembleProblem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_super_and_stage/#Super-individuals-and-stage-structure","page":"Super-individuals & stages","text":"Two extensions of the basic IBM: super-individuals (represent a large real population with few weighted particles while keeping exact aggregate counts) and stage structure (an individual-based finite-state continuous-time Markov chain).\n\nusing IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics\nusing IntegralProjectionModels\nusing StructuredPopulationCore: meshpoints, step_size\nusing Random, Statistics, LinearAlgebra, Plots","title":"Super-individuals and stage structure"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_super_and_stage/#Super-individuals","page":"Super-individuals & stages","text":"A super-individual carries a Weight — the number of real individuals it represents at one trait. Demographic events use exact aggregate draws (Binomial(w, survival) survivors, Poisson(w · fecundity) recruits), so a sum over particles is distributionally identical to tracking every individual, with far fewer entities.\n\nsurvival  = LinearSurvival(1.0, 0.0)\ngrowth    = NormalGrowth(0.5, 0.8, 0.4)\nfecundity = FecundityRate(-1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.3, 1.0)\ndomain    = ContinuousDomain(0.0, 6.0, 60)\nz = meshpoints(domain)\nh = step_size(domain)\n\nrng = Random.Xoshiro(31)\ntraits0 = clamp.(2.0 .+ 0.5 .* randn(rng, 20_000), 0.01, 5.99)\nn0 = zeros(Int, length(z))\nfor zt in traits0\n    n0[clamp(floor(Int, (zt - domain.lower) / h) + 1, 1, length(z))] += 1\nend\ntraits_s = [z[b] for b in 1:length(z) if n0[b] > 0]\nweights_s = [n0[b] for b in 1:length(z) if n0[b] > 0]\n\nworld = ibm_world_super(survival, growth, fecundity, domain;\n                        rng = rng, traits0 = traits_s, weights0 = weights_s)\n(total = total_count(world), particles = n_particles(world))\n(total = 20000, particles = 39)\n\nSo 20 000 individuals are carried by only ~60 particles, and the total tracks the deterministic IPM (merging back to the mesh each step to cap the particle count):\n\nres = ibm_run_super!(world, 4; merge_domain = domain)\n\nkernel = PKernel(survival, growth, domain) + FKernel(fecundity, domain)\ndetsol = solve(IPMProblem(kernel, domain, Float64.(n0), (0, 4)), DirectIteration())\ndet_total = [sum(u) for u in detsol.u]\n\nplot(0:4, det_total; label = \"deterministic IPM\", lw = 2)\nplot!(0:4, res.N; seriestype = :scatter, label = \"super-individual IBM\",\n      xlabel = \"time step\", ylabel = \"population size\",\n      title = \"20 000 individuals in ≤ 60 particles\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nBecause Binomial and Poisson draws sum exactly, the whole count distribution matches — not just the mean. With K = 200 particles of weight 50 (N = 10000), the one-step total has the analytic mean and variance of the full individual model:\n\nsconst, fconst = survival(0.0), exp(-1.0)\ntotals = map(1:400) do r\n    w = ibm_world_super(survival, growth, fecundity, domain;\n                        rng = Random.Xoshiro(1000 + r),\n                        traits0 = fill(2.0, 200), weights0 = fill(50, 200))\n    ibm_step_super!(w)\n    total_count(w)\nend\nN = 10_000\n(empirical = (mean(totals), var(totals)),\n analytic  = (N * (sconst + fconst), N * (sconst * (1 - sconst) + fconst)))\n(empirical = (10984.72, 5850.663258145365), analytic = (10989.38019801447, 5644.913744129241))","title":"Super-individuals"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_super_and_stage/#Stage-structure-(pure-jump-finite-state-IBM)","page":"Super-individuals & stages","text":"Individuals can instead carry a discrete Stage and jump between stages. Rates are a transition matrix Qtrans[s', s] (rate s to s), a per-stage death vector, and a birth matrix birth[to, from]:\n\nQtrans = [0.0 0.0 0.0;     # 1 -> 2 at 0.5, 2 -> 3 at 0.4 (juvenile -> sub-adult -> adult)\n          0.5 0.0 0.0;\n          0.0 0.4 0.0]\ndeath  = [0.1, 0.1, 0.2]\nbirth  = zeros(3, 3); birth[1, 3] = 0.6   # adults (stage 3) produce stage-1 offspring\n\nstages0 = vcat(fill(1, 10_000), fill(2, 6_000), fill(3, 4_000))\nsworld = ibm_world_stage(Qtrans, death, birth; rng = Random.Xoshiro(40), stages0 = stages0)\nsres = ibm_run_stage!(sworld, (0.0, 4.0); dt = 0.01, saveat = 0.5, n_stages = 3)\nsres.counts[1]\n3-element Vector{Int64}:\n 10000\n  6000\n  4000\n\nThe mean of this jump process is the finite-state generator dot n = G n. The per-stage counts track exp(Gt)n_0:\n\nG = [-0.6 0.0 0.6;\n      0.5 -0.5 0.0;\n      0.0 0.4 -0.2]\nn0_stage = Float64.([10_000, 6_000, 4_000])\nibm_counts = reduce(hcat, [Float64.(c) for c in sres.counts])'\ndet_counts = reduce(hcat, [exp(G .* t) * n0_stage for t in sres.t])'\n\nplot(sres.t, det_counts; lw = 2, label = [\"det. juv\" \"det. sub\" \"det. adult\"])\nplot!(sres.t, ibm_counts; seriestype = :scatter,\n      label = [\"IBM juv\" \"IBM sub\" \"IBM adult\"],\n      xlabel = \"time\", ylabel = \"count\",\n      title = \"Stage-structured IBM vs exp(Gt)·n₀\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=6}","title":"Stage structure (pure-jump finite-state IBM)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_continuous_time/#Continuous-time-dynamics-(measure-valued-PDMP)","page":"Continuous-time dynamics","text":"In continuous time, an individual's trait flows deterministically by a growth velocity v(z) between random birth and death events — a measure-valued piecewise-deterministic Markov process (PDMP). Its mean obeys the physiologically-structured population model (PSPM) transport equation,\n\nfracpartial npartial t = -fracpartialpartial zbig(v(z)nbig)\n  - mu(z)n + textbirths\n\nso this is the individual-level dual of the deterministic transport backend. An operator split (flow then jumps) over small steps handles it — no dedicated PDMP solver needed.\n\nThe continuous-time IBM is parameterized by per-individual rate functions of the trait: flow(x), mortality(x), fecundity(x), and recruit(rng).","title":"Continuous-time dynamics (measure-valued PDMP)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_continuous_time/#Birth–death-dynamics","page":"Continuous-time dynamics","text":"With no flow, constant birth rate b and death rate d, the mean grows as N_0 e^(b-d)t:\n\nusing IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics\nusing StructuredPopulationCore: ContinuousDomain\nusing Random, Statistics, Plots\n\nb, d = 0.6, 0.4\ndom = ContinuousDomain(0.0, 10.0, 50)\nworld = ibm_world_ct(x -> 0.0, x -> d, x -> b, rng -> 2.0, dom;\n                     rng = Random.Xoshiro(11), traits0 = fill(2.0, 5000))\nres = ibm_run_ct!(world, (0.0, 3.0); dt = 0.01, saveat = 0.25)\n\nplot(res.t, res.N; seriestype = :scatter, label = \"IBM\",\n     xlabel = \"time\", ylabel = \"population size\",\n     title = \"Birth–death: IBM vs N₀ exp((b−d)t)\")\nplot!(res.t, 5000 .* exp.((b - d) .* res.t); lw = 2, label = \"analytic mean\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}","title":"Birth–death dynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_continuous_time/#Advection-and-mortality","page":"Continuous-time dynamics","text":"Individuals flowing at constant velocity v and dying at rate mu, with no births: the population decays by mortality while the trait distribution advects rigidly, its mean following the characteristics bar z(t) = bar z(0) + v t:\n\nv, mu = 1.0, 0.2\ndom2 = ContinuousDomain(0.0, 50.0, 100)\nworld2 = ibm_world_ct(x -> v, x -> mu, x -> 0.0, rng -> 0.0, dom2;\n                      rng = Random.Xoshiro(22), traits0 = fill(5.0, 5000))\nres2 = ibm_run_ct!(world2, (0.0, 3.0); dt = 0.01, saveat = 0.5, save_traits = true)\n\nmean_traits = [mean(tr) for tr in res2.traits]\nplot(res2.t, mean_traits; seriestype = :scatter, label = \"IBM mean trait\",\n     xlabel = \"time\", ylabel = \"mean trait\",\n     title = \"Advection: mean trait follows the characteristics\")\nplot!(res2.t, 5.0 .+ v .* res2.t; lw = 2, label = \"5 + v·t\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nplot(res2.t, res2.N; seriestype = :scatter, label = \"IBM\",\n     xlabel = \"time\", ylabel = \"population size\",\n     title = \"Mortality decay: IBM vs N₀ exp(−μ t)\")\nplot!(res2.t, 5000 .* exp.(-mu .* res2.t); lw = 2, label = \"analytic\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}","title":"Advection and mortality"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_continuous_time/#A-size-structured-example","page":"Continuous-time dynamics","text":"Combining growth, death, and reproduction: individuals grow toward a maximum size, die at a constant rate, and large individuals reproduce (offspring entering near a fixed birth size) — the classic physiologically-structured IBM:\n\ndom3 = ContinuousDomain(0.0, 5.0, 100)\nflow(x)      = 0.6 * (5.0 - x)                 # von Bertalanffy-like growth\nmortality(x) = 0.1\nfecundity(x) = x > 2.0 ? 0.4 : 0.0             # only large individuals reproduce\nrecruit(rng) = 0.2 + 0.05 * randn(rng)         # offspring near size 0.2\n\nworld3 = ibm_world_ct(flow, mortality, fecundity, recruit, dom3;\n                      rng = Random.Xoshiro(5), traits0 = fill(0.5, 2000))\nres3 = ibm_run_ct!(world3, (0.0, 15.0); dt = 0.01, saveat = 5.0, save_traits = true)\n\nhistogram(res3.traits[end]; bins = 0:0.2:5, legend = false,\n          xlabel = \"size z\", ylabel = \"count\",\n          title = \"Stationary size distribution (t = 15)\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nplot(res3.t, res3.N; marker = :circle, legend = false,\n     xlabel = \"time\", ylabel = \"population size\",\n     title = \"Approach to a stationary size-structured population\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"A size-structured example"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_resilience/#Resilience-of-an-individual-based-population","page":"Resilience after a pulse","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nThe IPM and its resilience\nA pulse: removing every juvenile\nEstimating the return rate\nCaveats","title":"Resilience of an individual-based population"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_resilience/#Overview","page":"Resilience after a pulse","text":"After a disturbance, how fast does a structured population return to its usual composition? For the deterministic integral projection model (IPM) the answer is a number: the population converges to its stable size distribution at the rate ln(λ₁/|λ₂|) per step, from the two leading eigenvalues of the kernel. This is the resilience that ResilienceCore.jl computes for a projection matrix. The individual-based model (IBM) of this package is that IPM’s finite-N realisation. This vignette measures its return rate from simulations, and asks how close it comes to the IPM’s value and how this depends on population size.\n\nThe simulation-based measure is recovery from DynamicalResilience.jl. It works on any problem with SciML’s integrator interface. IBMProblem gives the IBM that interface: its state is the population binned on the IPM’s mesh, so IBM and IPM states can be compared directly.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics\nusing IntegralProjectionModels\nimport StructuredPopulationCore as SPC\nimport ResilienceCore\nusing DynamicalResilience: recovery\nusing LinearAlgebra, Plots, Random, Statistics","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_resilience/#The-IPM-and-its-resilience","page":"Resilience after a pulse","text":"The vital rates of the first vignette: survival that does not depend on size, growth towards 0.5 + 0.8z, and recruits of size about 1. Fecundity is set so that λ₁ ≈ 1. The population then neither grows nor declines, so populations of different sizes keep their size during a run.\n\ns = LinearSurvival(1.0, 0.0)                    # survival ≈ 0.731\ng = NormalGrowth(0.5, 0.8, 0.4)\nf = FecundityRate(-1.313, 0.0, 1.0, 0.3, 1.0)   # ≈ 0.269 recruits per individual\ndom = SPC.ContinuousDomain(0.0, 6.0, 60)\nkernel = PKernel(s, g, dom) + FKernel(f, dom)\nA = ResilienceCore.linearise(IPMProblem(kernel, dom, ones(dom.n_meshpoints), (0, 1)))\nev = sort(abs.(eigvals(Matrix(A.A))); rev=true)\nρ = ResilienceCore.resilience(A)\n(λ₁=round(ev[1]; digits=4), λ₂=round(ev[2]; digits=4), λ₃=round(ev[3]; digits=4),\n resilience=round(ρ; digits=3))\n\n(λ₁ = 0.9989, λ₂ = 0.5845, λ₃ = 0.4655, resilience = 0.536)\n\nThe IPM forgets a disturbance to its size distribution by a factor |λ₂|/λ₁ ≈ 0.59 per step. The third eigenvalue is not much smaller, so straight after a disturbance the return is somewhat faster than ρ.","title":"The IPM and its resilience"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_resilience/#A-pulse:-removing-every-juvenile","page":"Resilience after a pulse","text":"The population starts at the stable size distribution, scaled to N individuals. At step 5 every juvenile (z < 1.5) is removed, for example by a cohort failure. recovery runs the IBM twice, with and without the pulse, and records the distance between the two size distributions (proportions per bin). Before the pulse the second run replays the first exactly (reinit! restarts the random number generator). After it the two runs draw different random numbers, because the populations differ. So the distance does not fall to zero: it stops at a floor of sampling noise, of order 1/√N.\n\nvals, vecs = eigen(Matrix(A.A))\nw = abs.(real.(vecs[:, argmax(real.(vals))]))\nstable(N) = N .* w ./ sum(w)\nproportions(u) = u ./ max(sum(u), 1)\ncull(u, rng) = (v = copy(u); v[1:15] .= 0; v)\n\nNs = (200, 2_000, 20_000, 100_000)\nruns = Dict(N => [recovery(IBMProblem(VitalRates(s, g, f), stable(N), (0, 40); domain=dom,\n                                      seed), IBMStepping();\n                           perturb=cull, observe=proportions, t_pulse=5, horizon=30,\n                           interval=1) for seed in 1:5]\n            for N in Ns)\n\nfig = plot(; yscale=:log10, xlabel=\"steps after the pulse\",\n           ylabel=\"distance between size distributions\", legend=:topright,\n           ylims=(1e-3, 1), size=(720, 420))\nfor (k, N) in enumerate(Ns)\n    for r in runs[N]\n        plot!(fig, r.t, r.distance; color=k, alpha=0.5, label=\"\")\n    end\n    plot!(fig, [NaN], [NaN]; color=k, label=\"N = $N\")\nend\nd0 = mean(r.distance[1] for N in Ns for r in runs[N])\nplot!(fig, 0:30, d0 .* exp.(-ρ .* (0:30)); color=:black, ls=:dash, lw=2,\n      label=\"IPM: exp(−ρ t)\")\nfig\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe pulse moves the size distribution by 0.22 to 0.25 in every population. The distance then falls at about the IPM’s rate until it meets the noise floor: within a step or two for N = 200, and only after about 6 steps for N = 100 000.","title":"A pulse: removing every juvenile"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_resilience/#Estimating-the-return-rate","page":"Resilience after a pulse","text":"recovery fits the logarithm of the distance against time over a window of distances, given as fractions of the initial one. A window that reaches into the noise floor fits the floor, and the rate comes out near zero. The floor of each run is estimated from its last ten distances. The fit then uses only distances from the pulse itself down to twice the floor:\n\nfloor_of(r) = median(r.distance[(end - 9):end])\nfunction return_rate(r)\n    lo = 2 * floor_of(r) / r.distance[1]\n    lo < 1 || return NaN                         # the floor is as large as the pulse\n    keep = findall(d -> lo * r.distance[1] <= d <= r.distance[1], r.distance)\n    length(keep) >= 3 || return NaN\n    t, y = r.t[keep], log.(r.distance[keep])\n    return -cov(t, y) / var(t)\nend\nnaive(r) = r.return_rate                         # recovery's default window (0.1 to 1e-4)\n[(N=N, floor=round(mean(floor_of, runs[N]); sigdigits=2),\n  floor_times_sqrtN=round(mean(floor_of, runs[N]) * sqrt(N); digits=2),\n  rate_above_floor=round.(return_rate.(runs[N]); digits=2),\n  rate_default_window=round(mean(naive, runs[N]); digits=3)) for N in Ns]\n\n4-element Vector{@NamedTuple{N::Int64, floor::Float64, floor_times_sqrtN::Float64, rate_above_floor::Vector{Float64}, rate_default_window::Float64}}:\n (N = 200, floor = 0.13, floor_times_sqrtN = 1.87, rate_above_floor = [NaN, NaN, NaN, NaN, NaN], rate_default_window = NaN)\n (N = 2000, floor = 0.038, floor_times_sqrtN = 1.71, rate_above_floor = [NaN, NaN, NaN, NaN, NaN], rate_default_window = NaN)\n (N = 20000, floor = 0.012, floor_times_sqrtN = 1.69, rate_above_floor = [0.62, 0.66, 0.64, 0.68, 0.63], rate_default_window = 0.007)\n (N = 100000, floor = 0.0055, floor_times_sqrtN = 1.74, rate_above_floor = [0.6, 0.64, 0.65, 0.66, 0.59], rate_default_window = 0.016)\n\nThree things stand out:\n\nThe noise floor scales as 1/√N, at about 1.7/√N, as sampling noise between two populations of N individuals should.\nAbove the floor the IBM recovers at about the IPM’s rate. With 20 000 and 100 000 individuals the five estimates lie between 0.59 and 0.68, 10 to 25% above ρ ≈ 0.54. The fit starts at the pulse, where the faster third mode (ln(λ₁/|λ₃|) ≈ 0.76) still contributes. At N = 2 000 and N = 200 fewer than three distances lie above twice the floor, and there is nothing to fit.\nThe default window fails. recovery’s default window reaches down to 1e-4 of the pulse, deep into the floor. It returns no rate at all for the small populations and rates near zero (0.007 and 0.016) for the large ones. Choose the window from the floor, or average over replicates first.","title":"Estimating the return rate"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_resilience/#Caveats","page":"Resilience after a pulse","text":"The IBM here is density-independent, as is its IPM: “recovery” means recovery of the size distribution, not of a population size. Total numbers neither return nor diverge (λ₁ ≈ 1).\nThe common random numbers end at the pulse, because removing individuals changes the order in which the remaining ones draw random numbers. A coupling that kept the individuals’ own random streams would lower the floor.\nThe pulse acts at the resolution of the mesh: set_u! removes individuals bin by bin.","title":"Caveats"},{"category":"section","location":"#IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.jl","page":"Home","text":"Individual-based (agent / ECS) realizations of structured population models, built on the Ark.jl entity-component-system.\n\nEach individual is an entity carrying a continuous Size trait (and optionally a discrete Stage or a super-individual Weight); birth spawns an entity, death despawns one, and growth updates the trait. This is the finite-N \"ground truth\" whose large-N limit is the deterministic model:\n\nMode Realization Deterministic limit\nibm_world / ibm_step! discrete-time, size-structured the IPM n_t+1 = K n_t\nibm_world_ct / ibm_step_ct! continuous-time flow + jumps the PSPM transport / measure-valued PDMP\nibm_world_super / ibm_step_super! weighted super-individuals the same, with exact aggregate counts\nibm_world_stage / ibm_step_stage! discrete-stage pure jumps the finite-state generator dot n = G n\n\nVital-rate sampling is delegated to the StructuredPopulationCore sampler interface, whose analytic methods for the IntegralProjectionModels.jl vital-rate types are loaded automatically. A categorical specification can also be lowered to an individual-based realization via IBMStageTarget in CategoricalPopulationDynamics.jl.","title":"IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/ark-ecs/Ark.jl\")\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/ecorecipes/IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using IndividualBasedPopulationDynamics\nusing IntegralProjectionModels\nusing Random\n\nsurvival  = LinearSurvival(1.0, 0.0)\ngrowth    = NormalGrowth(0.5, 0.8, 0.4)\nfecundity = FecundityRate(-1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.3, 1.0)\ndomain    = ContinuousDomain(0.0, 6.0, 60)\n\nworld = ibm_world(survival, growth, fecundity, domain;\n                  rng = Random.Xoshiro(1), traits0 = fill(2.0, 1000))\nibm_run!(world, 5).N\n6-element Vector{Int64}:\n 1000\n 1082\n 1174\n 1304\n 1421\n 1532\n\nSee the tutorials for worked examples and the API reference for the full interface.","title":"Quick start"}]
}
